---
/**
 * Author + verifiable credentials, for pages a reader can land on straight from
 * a search result.
 *
 * The problem this solves: `/credentials/` is the strongest trust page on the
 * site, and somebody arriving on a blog post or a reference page from Google
 * never sees it. Google's quality guidance for advice that touches money,
 * relationships and major life decisions leans hard on a named author with
 * checkable training attached to the content itself — not filed away on a
 * separate page the reader has no reason to visit.
 *
 * Everything here is already stated on /credentials/. Nothing is added, inflated
 * or rounded up, and there is deliberately no "20 years of experience" style
 * claim, because no such figure has been supplied by the owner.
 *
 * `variant="full"` for long reads (blog, reference pages); `variant="compact"`
 * for short dated notes where a full card would outweigh the content.
 */
interface Props {
  variant?: 'full' | 'compact';
  /** Optional date line, already formatted. */
  dateline?: string;
}
const { variant = 'full', dateline } = Astro.props;
---

<aside class:list={['author-note', variant === 'compact' && 'author-note--compact']}>
  <p class="author-note__by">
    <span class="author-note__name">Written by Maureen “Mo” Lumen</span>
    {dateline && <span class="author-note__date"> · {dateline}</span>}
  </p>
  <p class="author-note__creds">
    Practitioner's Certificate in Horary Astrology (School of Traditional Astrology) · NCGR-PAA
    Levels 1 and 2 passed · Board member and treasurer, Organization for Professional Astrologers ·
    Member of NCGR, OPA and ISAR.
  </p>
  <p class="author-note__verify">
    Astrology is an unregulated field, so I'd rather you checked than took my word for it —
    <a href="/credentials/">every one of these is named and verifiable</a>.
  </p>
</aside>
